var tafsirData_70 = {
  "1": "\"A challenger has demanded a punishment bound to come\" opens Surah al-Ma‘arij with a request for immediate chastisement, echoing the deniers’ “When will this threat come if you are truthful?” The challenger is a Meccan denier, in tradition al-Nadr ibn al-Harith or similar, who said “O Allah, if this is truth, rain stones” or demanded punishment to test.\n\n“A challenger has demanded a punishment bound to come” translates “sa’ala sa’ilun bi-‘adhabin waqi‘,” a questioner asked about a punishment waqi', inevitable, waqi' as with the Waqi‘ah that will debase and elevate. Bound to come indicates certainty, as with the Inevitable Hour and the Striking Disaster denied by Thamud and ‘Ad. The demand is mocking, like “How can humans be guides?” and “Ancient fables!” It treats punishment as negotiable, as if the One in Whose Hands rests all authority could be challenged, similar to hypocrites who said honour will expel inferior.\n\nThe verse follows the glorify the Greatest command, showing that some, instead of glorifying, challenge.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think demanding punishment proves strength. It proves delusion that a powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate, and that provision will not be withheld.\n\nFor believers, the challenger’s demand is a warning not to demand signs on your terms, but to be patient with beautiful patience as commanded next, to listen and reason, and to prepare for the inevitable punishment that is bound to come for the persistent, while seeking the elevated Garden for the attentive, whose punishment is averted by faith and whose light shines ahead.\n\nThe punishment bound to come also frames the Beautiful patience commanded next. They see it as far, We see it as near, and the sky becomes molten brass while mountains become wool. The challenger who demanded a sign as if Allah could be goaded is answered not with immediate spectacle he can control, but with a promise that is bound to come from the Lord of pathways, a Lord whose pathways angels ascend on a Day fifty thousand years. Bound to come means written before creation where every calamity or blessing is written, so challenging does not hasten or avert, it only exposes the challenger as lost in delusion that a powerless force will help.",
  "2": "\"for the disbelievers—to be averted by none—\" qualifies the punishment demanded in the previous verse. The phrase removes any hope that the demanded punishment can be deflected.\n\n“for the disbelievers—to be averted by none—” translates “lil-kafirina laysa lahu dafi‘,” for the disbelievers there is no defender, no repeller. No defender echoes “which powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate? They are lost in delusion” and “who will provide if He withholds?” and “none could have shielded him from Us” in the hypothetical about the Messenger. No one, whether wealth, children, clan that sheltered, spouse, siblings, associate-gods, or oaths binding until the Day, can avert the inevitable punishment bound to come.\n\nFor the disbelievers indicates that the punishment is not for the mindful who are taught the pen, who are in awe without seeing, whose mighty reward is forgiveness, who give rightful share and guard chastity, but for those who persisted in denial as some will persist, whose regret will be hasrah.\n\n“to be averted by none” also clarifies that the challenger’s demand does not control timing. The punishment will come from Allah, Lord of pathways of ascent, on a Day fifty thousand years, whether demanded or mocked.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear “for the disbelievers” as predestination without choice. The Quran shows disbelievers chose, as Thamud and ‘Ad denied the Striking, as Pharaoh disobeyed the messenger, as garden owners withheld.\n\nFor reflection, ensure you are not among “for the disbelievers” by choosing the submitted path, listening and reasoning, walking upright, and being among those who pray consistently and give rightful share, so the punishment bound to come is averted for you through sincere repentance that perfects light, not through any defender besides the Most Compassionate in whom you believe and trust.\n\nNo defender also clarifies why wealth, children, clan that sheltered, spouses, siblings, and everyone on earth cannot ransom on that Day. The wicked wish to ransom with all of them, yet none can avert, as with the hypocrites whose oaths as shields became severe, humiliating punishment and whose wealth and children did not help against Allah. The only defender is the Most Compassionate in whom we believe and trust, the King, Holy, Almighty whose Hands hold authority. To have a defender on that Day, be among the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss, whose rightful share was given, whose families were protected, and whose light shines ahead, so the punishment for disbelievers averted by none is not for you.",
  "3": "\"from Allah, Lord of pathways of ˹heavenly˺ ascent\" identifies the source of the inevitable punishment that is for disbelievers and averted by none. The verse elevates the challenger’s demand to its true origin.\n\n“from Allah, Lord of pathways of heavenly ascent” translates “min Allah dhi al-ma‘arij,” Allah, Owner of the ascending ways. Ma‘arij are stairs, pathways, degrees by which angels and the holy spirit ascend, as next verse details on a Day fifty thousand years. Lord of pathways indicates He controls all ascents, including the command descending between seven heavens, the adorned lowest heaven with lamps, and the Throne borne by eight. Pathways also evoke the Hand that holds all authority, the Most Capable of everything, and the King, Holy, Mighty.\n\nFrom Allah emphasizes that punishment demanded is not from the Prophet’s invention, as hypothetical “Had he made up something We would have seized by the right hand and severed his aorta and none could shield” proved, but from the Lord of all worlds of whom the Quran is revelation.\n\nThis follows “for the disbelievers—to be averted by none,” showing whose punishment it is and why none can avert. No associate-gods can provide a pathway besides His, no scripture reads whatever you choose, no oath binds Him.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine pathways as literal stairs humans can climb to escape. Pathways are for angels and spirit, not for those who crawl facedown or withhold good.\n\nFor believers, Lord of pathways invites ascent through the ways He provided, reciting, purifying, teaching Book and wisdom, being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, protecting families, so ascent on the Day fifty thousand years is light, not punishment, and the challenger’s demanded punishment is not yours but the Inevitable Hour you prepared for with outstanding character and never-ending reward.\n\nLord of pathways also connects to the seven heavens created in layers, the lowest adorned with lamps as missiles against eavesdropping devils, and the Throne established. Pathways are not only angelic ascents but also the descending command between heavens that you may know He is Most Capable and encompasses all knowledge. The same pathways that carry angels on a Day fifty thousand years also carried the Reminder that is for the whole world, the Ark that carried you, and the provision from sources never imagined. To be Lord of pathways means every ascent and descent is owned, so the challenger’s demanded ascent of punishment cannot be directed, only awaited, and the believer’s ascent through hearing, obeying, and spending is guided.",
  "4": "\"˹through which˺ the angels and the ˹holy˺ spirit will ascend to Him on a Day fifty thousand years in length\" details the pathways of ascent introduced in the previous verse. The verse gives scale and participants of ascent.\n\n“through which the angels and the holy spirit will ascend to Him on a Day fifty thousand years in length” translates “ta‘ruju al-mala’ikatu wa al-ruhu ilayhi fi yawmin kana miqdaruhu khamsina alfa sanah,” angels and the spirit, Gabriel, ascend to Him on a Day whose measure is fifty thousand years. Ascend indicates the pathways are used by the obedient angels on sides of the torn sky and the eight bearing the Throne, who never disobey. Holy spirit, or “ruh,” is Gabriel, the noble angel who brings revelation, the same spirit breathed into Mary, now ascending with angels. The Day is the Inevitable Hour, the Striking, the Waqi‘ah when earth and mountains are crushed, the Trumpet blown, the sky torn.\n\nFifty thousand years is not human calculation to set, as with “When will this threat come?” and “That knowledge is with Allah alone,” but a measure of its gravity, length, and awe, similar to “We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend” and “My planning is flawless.” What seems long to the challenger who sees it as impossible, Allah sees as inevitable, and the deniers see as impossible while We see it as inevitable.\n\nThis follows Lord of pathways to show what pathways are and whose Day it is.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat fifty thousand years as literal human calendar to predict. It is measure of that Day’s magnitude, not a date to set.\n\nFor practice, the Day’s length should move you to beautiful patience next commanded, to walk upright, to give rightful share, and to protect families, so the long Day is for you like the light ahead and to the right, not like the demanded punishment bound to come for the disbelievers that none can avert, and your ascent with angels is with light perfected.",
  "5": "\"So endure ˹this denial, O Prophet,˺ with beautiful patience\" commands the Prophet after describing the challenger, the inevitable punishment for disbelievers averted by none from the Lord of pathways, and the Day fifty thousand years. The instruction is personal and model.\n\n“So endure this denial, O Prophet, with beautiful patience” translates “fa-sbir sabran jamilan,” be patient with a beautiful patience. Beautiful patience is forbearing without complaint, without hastening like Jonah the Man of the Whale who cried in distress, without retaliating like worthless planks, and without being cut down by eyes that would almost make you slip when you recite. Beautiful patience includes continuing to recite the Reminder to the whole world, to teach Book and wisdom, to make room, to lend good loans, and to leave rejecters to be gradually drawn in ways they cannot comprehend while flawless planning delays for a while.\n\nDenial here is the challenger’s demand, the “How can humans be guides?” mock, the “Ancient fables!” dismissal, and the “When will this threat come?” taunt. Enduring it is part of the test of best deeds for the Messenger of outstanding character whose never-ending reward is assured.\n\nThis follows the Day fifty thousand years to show that patience is needed because the Day is long in measure but inevitable, and the deniers see it as impossible while We see it as inevitable, so the Prophet must not be like Jonah in haste.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think beautiful patience is passive. It is active, struggling against disbelievers and hypocrites firmly where firmness belongs, protecting families, and delivering clearly.\n\nFor believers, beautiful patience is also the model when wealth and children test, when spouses or children act as enemies to remembrance, when provision is withheld, and when faces would be gloomy if you despaired. Endure with beautiful patience, remember often, and trust the Most Compassionate in whom we believe and trust, so your patience becomes part of the days gone by for which you will be told to eat and drink joyfully in the elevated Garden.",
  "6": "\"They truly see this ˹Day˺ as impossible\" reports the deniers’ perception after commanding beautiful patience. The verse contrasts their view with divine reality.\n\n“They truly see this Day as impossible” translates “innahum yarawnahu ba‘idan,” they see it as far, distant, unlikely. Far, or “ba‘id,” is not only temporal but psychological, they deem it improbable, like Thamud and ‘Ad denied the Striking, like those who said “We will not be resurrected” and “When we are dead and reduced to dust, will we really be resurrected and our forefathers?” and like those who felt secure that earth will not swallow or stones unleash. Seeing as far is delusion that a powerless force will help, that merchandise is better than what is with Allah, and that honour expels inferior.\n\nThis follows beautiful patience, indicating why patience is needed. The audience thinks the Day is far, so they mock the challenger’s demanded punishment as if it will never arrive, and they ask “When will this threat come?” with disdain.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think seeing as far is mere calendar error. It is moral distance, leading to persisting in arrogance and aversion, withholding good, and remaining lost in delusion.\n\nFor the patient, seeing as far is to be corrected by revelation that would humble a mountain, by the Ark reminder that attentive ears grasp, and by the Pen’s writing that never delays a soul when its appointed time comes. Let the far be seen as near through revelation, so you prepare before the Day the sky is like molten brass and mountains like wool, and your patience becomes beautiful because it is informed by nearness, not by impatience for signs.\n\nSeeing as far also explains why they persist in arrogance and aversion, withhold good, and call revelations ancient fables. Distance makes the Garden of Bliss seem like fantasy and the Blaze like metaphor, so merchandise fanfare draws crowds from the pulpit and garden owners whisper to exclude poor. The Quran corrects distance by showing the garden’s ashes by morning, the blast for Thamud, the wind for ‘Ad, and the crushing grip, all near, and by showing the Inevitable’s one blast and one crushing as near. For the patient, seeing as near is built by looking again at flawless heavens, seeing birds held, and remembering that to Him is your resurrection, so near becomes beautiful patience rather than beautiful delusion.",
  "7": "\"but We see it as inevitable\" gives the divine counterpart to “They see it as far.” The contrast is between human delusion and divine certainty.\n\n“but We see it as inevitable” translates “wa narahu qariban,” We see it as near, close, inevitable. Near, or “qarib,” is not only temporal nearness, but certainty and readiness, as with the Inevitable Hour, the Inevitable Event that will have come to pass, the Striking Disaster, and the punishment bound to come for disbelievers averted by none from the Lord of pathways. What they see as far, Allah sees as near because His command descends between seven heavens, His Hand holds all authority, His creation of seven heavens is without flaw, and His promise to inform you of what you did is easy.\n\nThis follows beautiful patience, showing patience is possible because the One who commands patience also guarantees nearness. The Prophet, told not to be like Jonah in haste, can be patient because the Day’s nearness is assured, as with the garden’s ashes by morning that came while they slept, and as with the horror cast on fortified hearts from where they never expected.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read nearness as immediate tomorrow, leading to date-setting. Near with Allah includes wise delay that draws gradually in ways they cannot comprehend, yet remains near in certainty.\n\nFor believers, seeing as near transforms conduct. You move about earth and eat provision knowing to Him is resurrection, you do not feel secure that earth will not swallow, you give rightful share, you protect families, you walk upright, and you are in awe without seeing, so near is not fear but hope, as your light ahead and to the right will meet the near Day as promotion, while the deniers’ far becomes the near they finally see with gloomy faces when torment draws near and they are told “This is what you claimed would never come.”",
  "8": "\"On that Day the sky will be like molten brass\" begins the description of the Day they see as far but We see as near. The verse uses a sensory simile for the heavens.\n\n“On that Day the sky will be like molten brass” translates “yawma takunu al-sama’ ka-al-muhl,” the sky will be like heated oil, molten metal, brass or oil dregs that are dark, hot, and turbulent. Sky here is the adorned lowest heaven with lamps that was flawless and you were told to look again without finding flaw, now melted, as earlier the sky will be torn frail and like molten brass, indicating the same Inevitable’s effect. Molten brass evokes heat, churning, and difficulty to look upon, opposite to the Pen’s clear writing.\n\nThis follows nearness to show what near looks like, and precedes mountains like tufts of wool, no close friend asking, and the wicked wishing to ransom with children and all on earth.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think molten brass is mere colour. It is state, the sky’s firmness becomes fluid, showing that even the most stable-seeming creation, like strongholds thought out of reach, is pliable before the Lord of pathways.\n\nFor reflection, if the sky, flawless and adorned, becomes like molten brass, then the merchandise fanfare, the honour boast, and the wealth and children that divert are even more molten. Let the sky’s future state make you present in awe, walk upright, and be among those whose light shines ahead, so molten sky is not your terror but your passage to the Throne borne by eight, to presentation with no secret hidden, and to the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach.\n\nMolten brass also mirrors the Fire’s own roar and fury that almost bursts, the raging Flame that rips scalps summoned for those who turned backs and hoarded. Sky as molten brass shows the heavens themselves become like the Fire’s fuel, indicating that on that Day the adorned lamps are no longer adornment but molten. The smoothed earth you were to move about and eat from is now molten sky above and wool mountains below, leaving no stable ground for the powerless force that would help. To avoid this sky, be among those in awe without seeing whose mighty reward is forgiveness, who are in awe of their Lord without seeing, who walk upright, so molten sky is for you a passage to the Throne, not a covering of disgrace.",
  "9": "\"and the mountains like ˹tufts of˺ wool\" continues the Day’s transformation after the sky becomes like molten brass. The mountains, symbols of firmness, become soft.\n\n“and the mountains like tufts of wool” translates “wa takunu al-jibalu ka-al-‘ihn,” mountains become like carded wool, fluffy, scattered, as earlier the Inevitable Event will debase and elevate, earth shaken violently, mountains crushed to pieces becoming scattered particles of dust, mountains lifted and crushed with one blow, and mountains like tufts of wool. Wool, or “‘ihn,” is dyed, fluffed wool that wind carries easily, showing that what seemed immovable becomes weightless before the Lord of pathways on a Day fifty thousand years.\n\nThis follows sky as molten brass to pair heavens and earth in undoing, both flawless creations now unmade, showing that neither height nor mass secures. Strongholds of fortified peoples, houses destroyed by their own hands, and the garden’s lushness reduced to ashes all foreshadow this.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as poetry without consequence. It is literal transformation that signals judgment where no secret stays hidden.\n\nFor the living, mountains like wool should humble, not terrify into inaction. Move about the smoothed earth now with thanks, eat provision with remembrance often, give rightful share, and walk upright, so the mountain you see today as flawless invites you to look again without finding flaw, and the mountain you will see like wool reminds you to build your deeds on the firmness that remains, the Throne borne by eight, the Pen’s truth, and the elevated Garden, not on the mountains that will be wool.\n\nTufts of wool also recall the scattered particles of dust from mountains crushed to pieces, the chaff after plants wither, and the ashes of the garden. Wool is light, dyed, and easily blown, showing total loss of mountain’s majesty that once impressed like planks leaned worthless that impress. Mountains like wool are the opposite of the seven heavens without flaw; the same Most Capable who created them flawless can make them wool. For the living, mountains like wool should make you consider who will provide if He withholds provision, and which powerless force will help, and to choose instead to lend the good loan that the Most Appreciative multiplies, so your mountain on that Day is not wool but the elevated Garden where you are held in honour.",
  "10": "\"And no close friend will ask ˹about˺ their friends\" describes social severance on the Day the sky is molten brass and mountains are wool. The verse shows isolation after cosmic upheaval.\n\n“And no close friend will ask about their friends” translates “wa la yas’alu hamimun hamima,” no intimate friend will ask about his intimate. Hamim is warm, close confidant, the same word as “no close friend” for the evildoer who will have no hamim, only pus. On the Day fifty thousand years, no hamim asks, though they will be made to see each other next, showing that seeing does not lead to helping. This is the social equivalent of the sky melting and mountains woolling, the human bond that was most trusted dissolves, as with hypocrites who promised to leave and help but never left, and as with wealth and children that will not help against Allah.\n\nThis follows sky and mountains to show that the Inevitable undoes not only cosmos but companionship, exposing delusion that a powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate and that honour belongs to tribe.\n\nThis precedes the wicked wishing to ransom themselves with children, spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth, showing that hamim not asking is the prelude to trying to ransom with those same people, offering them up when asking fails.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this denies friendship altogether. Close friendship exists in Gardens where thrones face face to face and mutual blame is replaced by hope for better garden, but on this Day for the wicked, hamim fails.\n\nFor practice, be the close friend now who does ask, who makes room, who prefers emigrants despite need, who protects families, and who encourages feeding the poor, so hamim you cultivate is the Party of Allah bound to succeed, whose Gardens are held in honour and who will have close friends there, not the hamim who will not ask on the Day the sky is brass.",
  "11": "\"˹although˺ they will be made to see each other. The wicked will wish to ransom themselves from the punishment of that Day by their children\" reveals the cruel visibility that follows friendlessness on the Day. The verse is poignant.\n\n“although they will be made to see each other” translates “yubassarunahum,” they will be shown one another, made to see. Hamim will see hamim, wicked will see children, spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth, but seeing does not help, as with keepers asking “Did a warner not come?” and they reply “Yes, but we denied,” seeing the warner then does not help.\n\n“The wicked will wish to ransom themselves from the punishment of that Day by their children” translates “yawaddu al-mujrimu law yaftadi min ‘adhabi yawma’idhin bi-banihi,” the criminal wishes he could ransom from that Day’s punishment with his sons. Children, earlier wealth and children diverting from remembrance and only a test, now become currency the wicked would spend, as with garden owners who would have given harvest to poor if they had said “Allah willing,” now they would give children to escape. The wish mirrors fool, as with those who thought honour expels inferior and merchandise is better than what is with Allah.\n\nThis follows no hamim asking to show that when asking fails, ransom is imagined, and precedes spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth, showing escalation.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think ransom is possible. The next verses say “But no! There will certainly be a raging Flame,” and earlier “So Today no ransom will be accepted” for hypocrites, and “We will inform you and that is easy for Allah.” Ransom is wished, not accepted.\n\nFor reflection, do not wait to offer children as ransom. Offer now what is accepted, the good loan multiplied and forgiven, the rightful share, the protection of families, the sincere repentance that perfects light, so the Day you see each other is not a Day you wish to ransom with your beloved, but a Day you are presented with record in right hand crying “Read my record” among those given light ahead.",
  "12": "\"their spouses, their siblings\" continues the wicked’s ransom wish after children. The list expands the currency from most beloved to nearest kin.\n\n“their spouses, their siblings” translates “wa sahibatihi wa akhihi,” his spouse and his brother. Spouses and siblings are the immediate family that smoothed earth life was shared with, the same spouses and children warned as enemies to you so beware, yet also those you are commanded to protect from Fire. The wicked, who in the world withheld good, compromised for yield, and obeyed slanderers, now would sacrifice the very intimates they once sought to please by prohibiting the lawful or by disclosing secrets out of affection for them, as with Hatib’s disclosure to protect family. The irony is stark. Those for whom they diverted from remembrance are now offered as ransom.\n\nThis follows children to show escalation. Children first, as most precious and as test, then spouses and siblings, the core household, then clan that sheltered, then everyone on earth, showing total desperation, as with wealth and children not helping and authority stripped, and no close friend asking.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this indicates family is worthless. Family is a test and a trust; the error is offering them as ransom instead of protecting them. The right use is to protect yourselves and families from Fire, not to offer them to Fire.\n\nFor the living, the escalation warns to invest in family rightly now. Love them, but do not let them divert from remembrance; provide according to means, consult courteously on nursing, and be mindful to best ability, so family is not wished as ransom but is among those you enter Gardens with, where light shines ahead and you are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by together.\n\nSpouses and siblings as ransom also show how the test of spouses and children as enemies to you so beware, yet pardon, overlook, forgive, is inverted. The wicked who let wealth or children divert from remembrance and are true losers now would divert spouses and siblings from punishment by offering them. True losers once diverted by them, now wish to ransom with them, showing diversion never ends well. The better way is to protect yourselves and families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones, to be mindful to best ability, and to be among those who give rightful share, so spouses and siblings are not ransom but companions in elevated Gardens where you recline face to face and are told to eat joyfully for days gone by, having turned to Him with hope for a better garden.",
  "13": "\"their clan that sheltered them\" adds the wider circle to the ransom wish after children, spouses, and siblings. The verse completes kinship before “everyone on earth.”\n\n“their clan that sheltered them” translates “wa fasilatihi allati tu’wihi,” his clan, his kin that gave refuge, that housed him, that he thought put him out of reach like strongholds. Fasilah is the tribe, the clan that sheltered, the people of insight’s opposite, who took a lesson after expulsion without horse or camel, but this wicked took no lesson. Shelter here is worldly shelter, the walls behind which hypocrites dare not fight except from fortified strongholds, and the merchandise honour that expels inferior.\n\nThe wicked would offer the very clan that sheltered him, showing that shelter was never true, as with the powerless force that will help instead of the Most Compassionate only delusion, and no defender can avert punishment for disbelievers from the Lord of pathways.\n\nThis follows spouses and siblings to show that when immediate family fails as ransom, the broader tribe is next, then everyone on earth, indicating total abandonment of all loyalties that were once placed before awe without seeing.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think clan loyalty is condemned. The Quran praises the clan of helpers who loved emigrants and preferred them, and the clan of the righteous whose Gardens are with their Lord; condemned is clan offered as ransom to escape personal accountability for withholding good and calling revelations ancient fables.\n\nFor practice, honour the clan that sheltered you by not making it your ransom. Use its shelter to be among those who give rightful share, are true to trusts, and walk upright, so clan is not offered up on the Day the sky is molten brass and mountains are wool, but is among those you hope to see in the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach and close friends are not absent.",
  "14": "\"and everyone on earth altogether, just to save themselves\" completes the ransom wish that began with children, spouses, siblings, and clan. After the wicked on the Day will be made to see each other and no close friend will ask, the Quran lists the final, most desperate offer.\n\n“and everyone on earth altogether, just to save themselves” translates the last item in the escalating list, “wa man fi al-ard jami‘an thumma yunjih,” and everyone on earth all together, then it would save him. The wicked, who hoarded wealth and turned backs, now imagines his ransom could include the entire planet’s population if it would buy his release from the raging Flame. “Just to save themselves” shows the self-centered core. Earlier he withheld good from the beggar and poor, now he would sacrifice everyone to save himself. The wish also mirrors Pharaoh’s indulged sin and the garden owners who would exclude the poor to save their harvest, now the logic is reversed.\n\nThis follows children, spouses, siblings, and clan that sheltered, showing total abandonment. It also follows the smoothed earth you were told to move about and eat from, and to Him is your resurrection, yet he would give away everyone on that same earth to avoid that resurrection’s consequence.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this is merely hyperbole. It is real desire, but the next verse says “But no! There will certainly be a raging Flame,” and earlier “So Today no ransom will be accepted” and “We will inform you and that is easy for Allah.” No ransom, even everyone on earth, will be accepted after persistent denial.\n\nFor the living, the lesson is to offer now what is accepted, the good loan multiplied, the rightful share, the protection of families, and sincere repentance that perfects light. Those are the ransoms that work before the Day the sky is molten brass and mountains are wool, because they are offered willingly in the days gone by for which you will be told to eat and drink joyfully, not desperately to save yourself when everyone on earth is seen and cannot help.",
  "15": "\"But no! There will certainly be a raging Flame\" answers the escalating ransom wish that ended with everyone on earth altogether to save themselves. The response is decisive rejection and vivid description.\n\n“But no!” translates “kalla,” an emphatic denial, stopping the fantasy that children, spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth could ransom. “There will certainly be a raging Flame” translates “innaha laza,” it is indeed a blazing Fire. Laza is a fiercely blazing Flame, one of the names of Hell, as with Hamim, Jahim, and Blaze that almost bursts. The Flame is described next as ripping off scalps and summoning those who turned backs and hoarded. It is the same Fire that almost bursts in fury, that roars as it boils over, that was prepared for eavesdropping devils and for those who disbelieve in their Lord.\n\nThis follows the ransom wish to show that wishing cannot change the Inevitable Hour, which they saw as far but We see as near, the Waqi‘ah that will have come to pass when Trumpet is blown with one blast and earth and mountains are crushed. The raging Flame is the answer to “who will provide if He withholds provision?” and “which powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate?” No one, and no ransom, provides escape from Laza.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear “raging Flame” as metaphor for regret. It is real, as with the garden reduced to ashes while they slept and the stones threatened from heaven, yet it is also the regret of those who said “if only we had listened.”\n\nFor the living, “But no!” should be heard now, before the Day the sky is like molten brass. Do not rely on the clan that sheltered you or everyone on earth to ransom you. Rely on being among those who pray consistently, give rightful share, believe firmly in the Day, fear punishment, guard chastity within lawful bonds, are true to trusts, honest in testimony, and properly observant, whose Gardens are held in honour and whose faces will be bright, not ransom seekers before a raging Flame.",
  "16": "\"ripping off scalps\" details the raging Flame just declared. The phrase gives a specific, visceral effect that matches the deniers’ history.\n\n“ripping off scalps” translates “nazza‘atan li-sh-shawa,” snatching away the scalp, the head’s skin, the extremities. Laza is described as nazza'ah, tearing off, as with the Fire that roars and almost bursts. Scalps, or “shawa,” are the outer limbs, the head’s skin, indicating that the Flame does not merely warm, but tears away what is most exposed and sensitive, as with the snout marked on the brute with wealth and children who said ancient fables, and as with the faces that become gloomy and are covered with disgrace.\n\nThis follows “There will certainly be a raging Flame” to show what that Flame does, and precedes “It will summon whoever turned their backs and turned away and gathered and hoarded,” showing who it calls. The ripping is fitting for those who turned backs, withholding good with slander and gossip, whose heads were turned away in arrogance when told to come for forgiveness.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to focus on graphic detail without moral link. Ripping scalps corresponds to the hard fixation on purpose that garden owners showed, totally fixated, and to being brute, despicable, withholder, and to feeling secure that One in heaven will not swallow or unleash stones. The scalp, the most prominent part, is ripped because prominence was used for boasting honour will expel inferior.\n\nFor reflection, the verse should not desensitize, but motivate protecting the head that will be asked to prostrate on the Day the Shin is bared. Use the head now to listen and reason, to look again at flawless heavens without finding flaw, to remember the Name of your Lord and devote yourself wholeheartedly, and to walk upright rather than crawl facedown, so the scalp that would be ripped is instead among bright faces looking at their Lord.",
  "17": "\"It will summon whoever turned their backs ˹on Allah˺ and turned away ˹from the truth˺\" explains whom the scalp-ripping Flame calls after stating it will certainly be a raging Flame. The summoned are defined by turning.\n\n“It will summon whoever turned their backs on Allah and turned away from the truth” translates “tad‘u man adbara wa tawalla,” it calls those who turned back and turned away. Adbara is to turn one’s back physically and morally, like hypocrites who turned heads in disgust when told to seek forgiveness, like the brute who turned his back and acted arrogantly after frowning, and like those who were called to prostrate when fully capable but chose not to. Tawalla is to turn away from following, to abandon, as with those who persisted in disbelief and turned away saying how can humans be guides, and those who turned away from remembrance of their Lord will be admitted into overwhelming punishment.\n\nThe inserted “on Allah” and “from the truth” clarify direction. Turning backs is against Allah who smoothed earth for you and holds birds, and turning away is from truth that would humble a mountain, that is reminder to those mindful, and that is the Straight Path walked upright.\n\nThis follows ripping scalps to show that scalps are ripped from those summoned, and follows the ransom wish to show who is not ransomed.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit turning away to open atheism. Turning away includes withholding good, transgressing, slandering, and feeling secure that One in heaven will not cause quaking or stones, persisting in arrogance and aversion.\n\nFor the living, avoid being summoned by turning to. Turn to Allah in sincere repentance so light is perfected, turn to the Messenger’s recitation that brings from darkness, turn to the Balance so people may administer justice, so the Flame that summons does not summon you, and the elevated Garden whose fruit hangs within reach summons you instead with “Eat and drink joyfully for what you did in days gone by.”",
  "18": "\"and gathered and hoarded ˹wealth˺\" adds the second trait of those summoned by the Flame, pairing turning away with hoarding.\n\n“and gathered and hoarded wealth” translates “wa jama‘a fa-aw‘a,” he gathered and contained, stored in vessels. Jama‘a is to collect, as with wealth and children blessed abundantly, with the garden owners’ “we will surely harvest all its fruit in the early morning,” with the deniers’ “we will leave with you” false promise, and with the Day He will gather you all for mutual loss and gain. Fa-aw‘a is to put into a container, to hoard in vaults, to withhold from circulation, opposite to the gains for Allah and the Messenger, orphans, poor, needy travellers so wealth does not merely circulate among your rich, and opposite to the good loan multiplied.\n\nThe inserted “wealth” clarifies. The Quran elsewhere says gathered and hoarded wealth is for those who withhold rightful share, who are despicable withholders of good, who think honour expels inferior, and who are lost in delusion thinking a powerless force will help.\n\nThis follows turned backs and turned away to show hoarding is the practical expression of turning, as with wealth diverting from remembrance making true losers and wealth merely a test with great reward only with Allah.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to condemn saving per se. The Quran praises providing according to means, dispersing to seek bounty after Friday, and moving about regions to eat provision, all of which require saving, but condemns gathering that hoards and withholds the poor’s entry today.\n\nFor practice, gather and release. Gather provision lawfully, then hoard not, but spend as good loan, give rightful share, make room, and let poor enter your garden today, so the Flame that summons gatherers and hoarders does not summon you, and the raging Flame is replaced by the higher truth that those who pray consistently and give and are true to trusts are held in honour in Gardens.",
  "19": "\"Indeed, humankind was created impatient\" diagnoses the root after describing the Flame that summons hoarders. The verse steps back to creation.\n\n“Indeed, humankind was created impatient” translates “inna al-insana khuliqa halu‘an,” the human is created anxious, hasty, greedy. Halu‘ is impatience that swings between distress when touched with evil and withholding when touched with good, as next verses define. Created impatient indicates a disposition, not a final destiny, as with death and life created to test which is best in deeds, and hearing, sight, intellect hardly thanked. Impatience explains why some demand punishment bound to come immediately like the challenger, why they see the Day as far, why they proceed early fixated on purpose, and why they would ransom with everyone on earth.\n\nThis follows hoarding to show hoarding is impatience’s fruit. Hoarding is impatience facing good, withholding instead of sharing.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use creation as excuse, as if impatience excuses hoarding and turning. The Quran says impatience is created, but then lists exceptions, those who pray and guard, who overcome it through discipline, as with mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying.\n\nFor reflection, impatience is to be managed, not indulged. The test is to become among the excepted, who pray and give, so the impatient creation is refined into the patient who endures denial with beautiful patience as commanded, who is not like Jonah in distress, and who walks upright, so impatience does not lead to the raging Flame that rips scalps but to Gardens held in honour where impatience is healed.\n\nThis impatience also explains the garden owners’ midnight pact without “Allah willing,” the hypocrites’ boycott “do not spend so they break away,” and the brute’s “we will surely harvest all.” In each case, impatience makes the future feel guaranteed, so gratitude and sharing feel optional. The Quran counters by making the future explicitly contingent on the Most Compassionate who holds birds, smooths earth, and can make water sink. Recognizing impatience as created, not chosen, invites training, not shame. Training is the rest of the list: consistent prayer, rightful share, belief in the Day, fear that knows none should feel secure, and guarding chastity within lawful bonds, all of which turn anxious haste into measured trust.",
  "20": "\"distressed when touched with evil\" explains one pole of impatience. The verse defines halu‘ through reaction to hardship.\n\n“distressed when touched with evil” translates “idha massahu al-sharru jazu‘an,” when evil touches him, he is anguished, fretful. Evil here is calamity, loss, harm, like the garden reduced to ashes while they slept, earth quaking violently, stones unleashed, or personal touch like illness, loss of wealth, or death that never delays. Distressed, or “jazu‘,” is impatient grief, wailing, despairing, like the deniers’ gloom when torment draws near and the garden owners’ “We must have lost our way!” before “We have been deprived.” It is the opposite of beautiful patience, of faith that guides hearts through adversity when calamity befalls except by Allah’s Will, and of beautiful trust in the Most Compassionate.\n\nThis follows “humankind was created impatient” to show that impatience appears first as distress, similar to those who feel secure until swallowing then know how serious warning was only after.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think distress is sinful. Distress is human; impatient distress is the excess that forgets the One who smoothed earth, who provides from sources never imagined, and who makes a way out for the mindful.\n\nFor practice, when touched with evil, be among those who pray, who fear punishment knowing none should feel secure, and who put trust in Allah, the Most Subtle, All-Aware who knows whether you speak secretly or openly. Distress then becomes supplication that perfects light, not the gloom that covers downcast eyes, and impatience is healed into patience that endures on the Day the sky is like molten brass.\n\nDistress when touched with evil also tests beauty. Impatience turns distress into the gloom that covers faces when torment draws near, into eyes downcast totally covered with disgrace when asked to prostrate, and into the cry “I wish I had not been given my record.” The excepted, by contrast, are taught to meet evil with prayer that is consistent and properly observed, with the rightful share that continues even when provision is limited, and with the knowledge that punishment is not to be felt safe from. That fear does not increase distress, but channels it into supplication that perfects light, as with the garden owners’ final “Glory be to our Lord, we have been wrongdoers” and the hopeful “Our Lord will give us better.”",
  "21": "\"and withholding when touched with good—\" explains the other pole of impatience, the reaction to blessing.\n\n“and withholding when touched with good—” translates “wa idha massahu al-khayru manu‘an,” when good touches him, he is withholding, niggardly. Good here is provision, wealth, health, children, gardens, rivers, the bounty after hardship, the excellent provision granted to those who believe and do good. Withholding, or “manu‘,” is to prevent good from reaching others, as with “Do not let any poor enter your garden today” and “Do not spend on those with the Messenger so they will break away,” and as with the despicable withholder of good, transgressor, evildoer. The dash indicates the thought pauses before the exception that follows, emphasizing that withholding is the natural impatient response to good, not sharing.\n\nThis follows distressed when touched with evil to show impatience swings: grasping in hardship, hoarding in ease, both diverting from remembrance and making true losers, as with wealth and children diverting.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think withholding is prudent saving. The Quran distinguishes providing according to means, where even limited resources give what Allah has given and after hardship He brings ease, from withholding that makes wealth merely circulate among rich.\n\nFor reflection, when touched with good, be among the excepted who give rightful share to beggar and poor, who lend good loan multiplied and forgiven by the Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing, and who are true to trusts. Withholding then becomes giving, and good becomes gratitude that uses hearing, sight, intellect to thank, walking upright on the Straight Path, so good does not become the withholder’s chain of seventy arms but the near fruit that hangs within reach in the elevated Garden.\n\nWithholding when touched with good also connects to the broader economy of delusion: “which powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate? They are lost in delusion,” and “who will provide if He withholds? They persist in arrogance and aversion.” Withholding sustains delusion that provision is self-made, so the withholder feels secure until the garden is ashes by morning. The exception breaks delusion by giving a known right to the asker and the deprived, a right that is known because it is measured, witnessed by two reliable men in divorce, and preserved by the pen. That known right makes provision a trust, not a trophy, so good becomes thanks and the Garden’s near fruit becomes reachable.",
  "22": "\"except those who pray,\" begins the exception to impatience’s distress and withholding. The verse lifts a group out of halu‘.\n\n“except those who pray,” translates “illa al-musallin,” save those who perform prayers. Prayer is the stabilizer for impatient creation, as with night worship that is more impactful and suitable for recitation, and weighty revelation soon sent, and day over-occupied. Those who pray are not distressed to despair nor withholding to hoard, because prayer connects them to the Lord of pathways on a Day fifty thousand years, to the Knower of seen and unseen, and to the Most Capable who holds birds.\n\nThis follows withholding when touched with good dash, showing that prayer is the first breaker of impatience. Prayer was also the consistent trait of those in Gardens held in honour and properly observant, and of those whose light shines ahead and to the right after sincere repentance.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit pray to occasional ritual without consistency or mindfulness, as with saying without doing that is despicable. The next verses expand consistently performing prayers and being properly observant, showing prayer must be regular and mindful.\n\nFor practice, be among those who pray to exit impatience. Stand for nearly two-thirds of the night or half or less as the Prophet did, remember the Name of the Lord and devote yourself wholeheartedly, be patient with what they say and depart courteously, so distress becomes patience and withholding becomes giving, and the raging Flame that rips scalps and summons hoarders does not summon you, but Gardens held in honour do, where you are told to eat and drink joyfully.\n\nPrayer as the exception also recalls the weighty revelation soon sent, night worship more impactful, and day over-occupied. The Messenger who stands for nearly two-thirds of the night, half or less or more, reciting properly, models the prayer that heals impatience. It is night worship that steadied him after being hurt like Moses was hurt, after the pen’s oath and outstanding character, before the difficult Day far from easy for disbelievers. For the community, excepted prayer is both the nightly measured recitation and the Friday call where you proceed diligently and leave business, then disperse to seek bounty and remember often to be successful. Both heal halu‘.",
  "23": "\"consistently performing their prayers;\" qualifies those who pray, showing that exception to impatience requires steadiness.\n\n“consistently performing their prayers;” translates “alladhina hum ‘ala salatihim da’imun,” those who are upon their prayers always, constant. Da’imun indicates perseverance, not occasional performance, like the Messenger who stands for nearly two-thirds of the night, half or a little less or more, reciting properly in a measured way, and like those properly observant of prayers at the end of the list. Consistency contrasts with those who pray only when seen, who are like planks that impress and think every cry against them, and with hypocrites who turn heads from forgiveness.\n\nThis follows “except those who pray,” adding that the exception is not a single prayer, but a habit that steadies impatience. Consistently praying counters being distressed when touched with evil, because regular remembrance habituates trust, and counters withholding when touched with good, because regular prayer includes zakah and rightful share.\n\nThe verse precedes giving rightful share, believing firmly in the Day, fearing punishment, and guarding chastity, showing prayer as foundation for the ethical chain that follows.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to equate consistency with mechanical repetition. Consistent prayer here is mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, as with being mindful to best ability and saved from selfishness.\n\nFor daily life, pray consistently, as those who pray are the first excepted from the raging Flame that summons hoarders. Let the call on Friday be answered diligently and daily prayers be kept, so impatience is replaced by beautiful patience with the Lord’s decree, not like Jonah in distress, and so the Day the sky is molten brass finds you with light perfected, not with scalp ripped.\n\nConsistent prayer also connects to the earlier ethic of making room when told and rising when told for which Allah elevates the faithful and raises the knowledgeable in rank. Consistency is a form of making room in time, sliding worldly duties to make space for prayer as helpers slid to make room for emigrants, and as the Pen makes space for truth. The All-Aware of what you do sees that spatial and temporal generosity and elevates those who are faithful and knowledgeable. That elevation is the opposite of the raging Flame that summons hoarders, and it places the consistent in Gardens held in honour where impatience is finally calmed.",
  "24": "\"and who give the rightful share of their wealth\" adds the second trait of the excepted after consistent prayer. The verse pairs worship with wealth.\n\n“and who give the rightful share of their wealth” translates “wa alladhina fi amwalihim haqqun ma‘lum,” those in whose wealth is a known right. Right, or “haqq,” indicates an obligation, not optional excess, and known, or “ma‘lum,” indicates it is specified, as with gains for orphans, poor, needy travellers so wealth does not circulate among rich, and as with provision according to means for waiting women, and as with zakah. Giving rightful share counters withholding when touched with good, the halu‘ pole, and counters gathered and hoarded wealth that the Flame summons.\n\nThis follows consistent prayer to show that prayer without wealth sharing is incomplete, as with the garden owners who prayed not, excluded the poor, and found ashes, and as with the hypocrites who said do not spend on those with the Messenger.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think rightful share is only zakah at a fixed rate. Rightful share includes zakah and additional known rights like feeding sixty poor where required, compensating nursing, and lending the good loan.\n\nFor practice, assign a known right in your wealth, a portion for beggar and poor, and give it regularly as you pray regularly, so impatience that withholds becomes the excepted who are true to trusts and whose Gardens are held in honour, where fruit hangs within reach because you let the poor enter your garden today.\n\nRightful share also echoes the gain distribution so wealth does not merely circulate among the rich and the man of wealth providing according to means while the limited provides from what Allah gave, after hardship easing. The known right makes the share predictable, so the asker and deprived can plan, and the giver can be saved from selfishness and be truly successful. It also parallels the two witnesses called to bear true testimony for Allah, both specifying what is known and preserved. Giving the known right turns the dash after “withholding when touched with good—” into the start of virtue, where good is not an end but a means to be mindful to best ability, hear, obey, and spend, which is best for you.",
  "25": "\"to the beggar and the poor;\" specifies who receives the rightful share just mentioned. The recipients are named with dignity.\n\n“to the beggar and the poor;” translates “li-l-sa’il wa al-mahrum,” for the asker and the deprived. Asker, or “sa’il,” is the one who asks openly, perhaps at the garden gate or after Friday dispersal seeking bounty; deprived, or “mahrum,” is the one who does not ask out of dignity, but is deprived, like the emigrants driven from homes seeking bounty, the waiting woman in need, and the needy traveller. Both have a known right, showing that wealth is entrusted to include the silent as well as the vocal.\n\nThis follows rightful share to clarify that sharing is not to the honourable who will expel the inferior, but to the beggar and poor the garden owners excluded. It mirrors the gain distribution so wealth does not merely circulate among rich and the way out and provision from sources never imagined for the mindful.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit giving to those who ask persuasively, overlooking the deprived who do not ask, or to give only to impress. Rightful share to the deprived requires looking again, as with flawless heavens, to see the hidden need.\n\nFor reflection, when touched with good, withhold not but give a known right to both asker and deprived. Let the beggar enter your garden today and seek the deprived who does not ask, so the halu‘ that withholds is healed, and you are among those held in honour in Gardens where you are told to eat and drink joyfully for what you did in days gone by, where no one is deprived as you once deprived.\n\nBeggar and deprived also embody the earlier test of hospitality. The beggar who asks at the garden gate tests whether “Do not let any poor enter today” will be whispered, and the deprived who does not ask tests whether the smooth earth you move about is seen as yours or His. The Quran’s earlier command to let the poor enter today and to encourage feeding the poor, not just feeding when asked, is fulfilled by naming both. The deprived’s silence is not consent to be ignored; it is a trial of attentiveness, as with attentive ears that grasp the Ark reminder and the pen that writes what everyone writes, both noticing what is not loud.",
  "26": "\"and who ˹firmly˺ believe in the Day of Judgment;\" adds the third trait of the excepted, anchoring prayer and sharing in accountability.\n\n“and who firmly believe in the Day of Judgment;” translates “wa alladhina yusaddiquna bi-yawm al-din,” those who affirm the Day of recompense, the mutual loss and gain, the Inevitable Hour, the Striking Disaster, the Day the sky is molten brass and mountains are wool, when no close friend will ask and the wicked wish to ransom with everyone on earth. Firmly, or “yusaddiquna,” is to confirm as true, not to treat as far, as deniers do, nor to demand its date as challenger did, but to live as if it is near, as We see it as near.\n\nThis follows rightful share to show that sharing is motivated by belief in recompense, as with the garden owners who feared no recompense until ashes, and the hypocrites who thought every cry against them but not the Keeper’s cry.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce belief to mental assent. Firm belief appears as knowing none should feel secure from punishment, fearing it, guarding chastity, and being true to trusts.\n\nFor practice, firmly believe so that impatience that is distressed with evil and withholding with good is steadied by the Day you will be presented and no secret hidden, and given your record, so you become among those who knew they would face reckoning and said “Read my record” in a life of bliss, rather than those who wished they had not been given their record and found wealth and authority stripped.\n\nFirm belief in the Day also steadies the earlier question “They see it as far, We see it as near,” and the challenger who demanded punishment bound to come as if far. Firm belief brings near, so impatience that demands immediacy is calmed. Near belief makes you walk upright on the Straight Path, not crawl facedown, and makes you among those who protect families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones. It also aligns with the severe account for rebellious societies and the horrible punishment tasted, making far seem near enough to act today, where sincere repentance perfects light and the Inevitable has not yet come to pass but is prepared for.",
  "27": "\"and those who fear the punishment of their Lord—\" continues the traits of the excepted, adding reverent fear after firm belief.\n\n“and those who fear the punishment of their Lord—” translates “wa alladhina hum min ‘adhabi rabbihim mushfiqun,” those who are apprehensive of their Lord’s punishment. Mushfiqun is cautious fear, awe that makes one careful, like those in awe without seeing who have forgiveness and mighty reward, and like the wife of Pharaoh who prayed for deliverance and Mary who guarded chastity and testified devoutly. Fear here is not paralyzing dread that forgets that Allah is Most Compassionate in whom we believe and trust, but the fear that makes one protect families from Fire whose fuel is people and stones.\n\nThis follows firmly believing in the Day to show belief produces fear, and precedes “knowing that none should feel secure,” which explains why.\n\nFear of punishment counters the feeling of security that the One in heaven will not cause earth to swallow or stones to unleash, and counters delusion that a powerless force will help.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think fear contradicts trust or hope for better garden. Fear and hope are paired, as with “Our Lord will give us better, for we are turning with hope” after woe, and with beautiful patience that trusts.\n\nFor daily life, fear punishment by avoiding its causes, the despicable oath-taking, slander, withholding, transgressing, brute arrogance that says ancient fables, and fixated proceeding without “Allah willing.” Replace them with mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, spending, and being saved from selfishness, so fear becomes the guard that keeps you from the raging Flame that rips scalps and that summons hoarders, and instead holds you in Gardens held in honour.\n\nFear of the Lord’s punishment also completes the earlier warning “Do you feel secure that One in heaven will not cause earth to swallow as it quakes?” and “unleash stones.” Those who feel secure are not among the excepted; they are lost in delusion. Fear that is mushfiq is vigilant, like the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey, but for good, watching trusts. That fear does not make you think every cry is against you like worthless planks, but makes you hear the Fire’s roar as warning while still in the world, so you turn before being tossed and hearing it as reality.",
  "28": "\"˹knowing that˺ none should feel secure from their Lord’s punishment—\" explains the fear just stated. The dash indicates a parenthetical awareness that sustains fear.\n\n“knowing that none should feel secure from their Lord’s punishment—” translates “inna ‘adhaba rabbihim ghayru ma’mun,” indeed their Lord’s punishment is not to be felt safe from. Not feeling secure is the opposite of the challenger who demanded punishment as if it were negotiable, of those who felt secure that earth will not swallow or stones unleash, and of the hypocrites who thought every cry was against them but not the true cry. Security from punishment is an illusion that makes true losers diverted by wealth and children.\n\nThis follows those who fear to show that fear is based on knowledge, not anxiety. They know the punishment is not to be felt safe from, as with the formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey, the shackles, raging Fire, choking food, and painful punishment in store, the earth and mountains shaking, the sky splitting.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to turn “none should feel secure” into despair that denies the Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing who multiplies good loans and forgives, and the way out for the mindful. Not feeling secure does not mean feeling hopeless; it means not dismissing warning as ancient fables.\n\nFor practice, hold both, fear that avoids withholding and distress, and hope that perfects light and expects Gardens under which rivers flow to stay for ever and ever, excellent provision. That balanced fear keeps you among those who pray consistently and guard chastity, whose Gardens are held in honour, not among those the raging Flame summons.\n\nKnowing none should feel secure also ties to the beautiful patience commanded. The Prophet endures denial with beautiful patience precisely because he knows punishment is not to be felt safe from, yet also knows that mercy is with forgiveness and mighty reward for those in awe unseen. Beautiful patience is not complacency that feels secure; it is endurance that knows the Day fifty thousand years is real, the sky molten brass, mountains wool, and no hamim asking, yet continues to recite the Reminder to the whole world with outstanding character, leaving the gradual drawing of rejecters to the One whose planning is flawless and who delays for a while.",
  "29": "\"and those who guard their chastity\" adds the next trait of the excepted, moving from fear of punishment to purity in private life.\n\n“and those who guard their chastity” translates “wa alladhina hum li-furujihim hafizun,” those who protect their private parts. Guarding includes the pledge of believing women not to fornicate nor falsely attribute illegitimate children, and the example of Mary who guarded her chastity and was devout, and the command to protect families from Fire. Guarding counters the brute’s withholder, transgressor, evildoer, and the garden owners’ fixated purpose that included no room for accountability.\n\nThis follows fearing punishment, showing that reverent fear produces chastity, and precedes the exception for wives and bondwomen, which defines the lawful outlet, and the warning that seeking beyond is transgression.\n\nChastity counters the impatient withholding when touched with good that also misuses good for illicit pleasure, and the distress when touched with evil that seeks forbidden relief.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce guarding to mere abstinence without context of trust, provision according to means, and courteous consultation for nursing that the Quran pairs with chastity.\n\nFor daily guarding, be true to trusts and covenants, honest in testimony, and properly observant of prayers, so guarding is not isolated but part of the solid ranks loved by Allah like a concrete structure. That guarding places you among those held in honour in Gardens, where splendid homes await and where the raging Flame that summons hoarders does not summon the chaste who feared punishment and knew none should feel secure.\n\nGuarding chastity also protects the lineage that ransom wish would sacrifice. The wicked wish to ransom with children, spouses, siblings, clan, and everyone on earth, but the excepted protect chastity to build family as a trust, not a ransom. Guarding includes the wives who turn in repentance after faltering, who become submissive, faithful, devout, repentant, worshipping, fasting, whether previously married or virgins, and Mary who guarded chastity and testified devoutly. Guarding is thus not isolated purity, but family building that shelters from the swallowing earth and unleashed stones, and that produces the better garden hoped for after turning.",
  "30": "\"except with their wives or those ˹bondwomen˺ in their possession, for then they are free from blame,\" defines the lawful scope for the chastity guarding just praised. The verse gives the permitted outlet.\n\n“except with their wives or those bondwomen in their possession, for then they are free from blame” translates the historical lawful relationships recognized in that context. Wives are the marriage bond made with witnesses and honourable retaining or separating, with housing according to means, compensation for nursing, and courteous consultation. Bondwomen, or “ma malakat aymanuhum,” refers to a historical category of female slaves whose status was regulated toward eventual emancipation, with freeing slaves as expiation for zihar and other sins, indicating a trajectory toward liberation, not endorsement of perpetual slavery. Free from blame indicates that intimacy within these lawful bounds is not transgression, unlike seeking beyond them.\n\nThis follows guarding chastity to show that guarding is not celibacy, as monasticism they invented was not ordained, but lawful, compassionate intimacy within limits set by Allah, whose limits transgressed wrongs one’s soul.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to apply bondwomen today as ongoing permission without historical context and without the Quran’s strong push toward freeing, or to use this verse to justify harshness. The Quran’s broader ethic protects families from Fire, encourages marriage, and makes freeing a central good, indicating that the moral direction is toward dignity and liberation.\n\nFor practice, guard chastity by honoring marriage as the Prophet did with outstanding character, by protecting families, by being true to trusts, and by seeking lawful, consensual marriage with honour, so you are not among transgressors beyond, but among those properly observant, held in honour in Gardens where elevated furnishings and near fruit await the pure.\n\nWives or bondwomen in possession also recalls the trajectory toward freeing, as with expiation by freeing a slave before touching, fasting two months, or feeding sixty poor, and as with gains not merely circulating among rich. Freeing is the Pen’s preferred writing. The Quran’s mention of bondwomen in this historical context is paired with strong encouragement to free, to make the “free from blame” within lawful bounds a step toward freeing from blame altogether by freeing the bound. For the excepted, lawful intimacy is free from blame, but seeking beyond remains transgression, so guarding stays within the smoothed earth’s lawful pathways, not the unleashed stones’ path.",
  "31": "\"but whoever seeks beyond that are the transgressors.\" sets the boundary after defining the lawful exception for chastity. The verse names those who exceed.\n\n“but whoever seeks beyond that are the transgressors” translates “fa-man ibtagha wara’a dhalika fa-ula’ika humu al-‘adun,” who seeks beyond wives and lawful bondwomen are the crossers, the transgressors. Transgressors, or “‘adun,” exceed limits set by Allah, as with societies that rebelled and were called to severe account, with the despicable transgressor, evildoer, and brute, and with those who felt secure that earth will not swallow.\n\nThis follows the lawful exception to show that guarding is not vague, but has a clear protected zone. Beyond that is not mere mistake, but transgression, like the garden owners who went totally fixated beyond “Allah willing,” and like those who wished to compromise the Reminder to extinguish light.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat this as harsh judgment on human desire itself. Desire is recognized as test, as wealth and children are test, and lawful provision is given to fulfill it with blessing.\n\nFor daily guarding, recognize that seeking beyond lawful wives and historical bondwomen is transgression that leads away from the raging Flame’s summons? Rather, it leads to it. To be among the excepted held in honour, guard within lawful bounds, be mindful to best ability, hear and obey, spend, and be among those in Gardens where you are told to eat and drink joyfully, where seeking beyond is unnecessary because provision is excellent and near.\n\nTransgressors also include those who exceed the pen’s writing and the well-preserved Record’s limits, who say “we will have whatever we choose” in a scripture they claim, or “oaths binding until the Day whatever we decide,” or who have associate-gods to bring forth. Exceeding wives and bondwomen is the intimate form of the same excess that withholds good and says ancient fables. The excepted, by staying within, are not deprived; they are held in honour in Gardens where splendid homes and near fruit are theirs, where seeking beyond is unnecessary because what is within is excellent provision granted by Him, and where the raging Flame that summons transgressors has no summon for them.",
  "32": "\"˹The faithful are˺ also those who are true to their trusts and covenants;\" adds the next trait of the excepted, extending chastity to fidelity.\n\n“The faithful are also those who are true to their trusts and covenants;” translates “wa alladhina hum li-amanatihim wa ‘ahdihim ra‘un,” those who are shepherds, guardians, over their trusts and promises. Trusts, or “amanat,” include the Reminder that would humble a mountain, the well-preserved Record only purified touch, the pen’s writing, the gains not to circulate only among rich, the divorce limits counted accurately, the two witnesses bearing true testimony for Allah, and family trusts like nursing consultation courteously. Covenants, or “‘ahd,” include the covenant already taken, the pledge of believing women not to associate, steal, fornicate, kill children, falsely attribute, nor disobey in what is right, and the covenant to hear and obey.\n\nBeing true, or “ra‘un,” is to watch, guard, as with formidable severe angels over the Fire who never disobey, but here for good.\n\nThis follows chastity to show that guarding private parts and guarding trusts are parallel, both protecting limits, and precedes honesty in testimony, which is a specific trust.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit trusts to money. Trusts include knowledge, family, and community health, as with the wives who were told it will be better if they turn in repentance after disclosing the Prophet’s confidence, and the hypocrites who disclosed secrets to pagans out of affection.\n\nFor practice, be true to trusts, keep the good loan, make room when told, leave business for Friday, and deliver messages as the noble Messenger does, so you are among those properly observant whose Gardens are held in honour, and the raging Flame that summons hoarders does not summon the trustworthy.\n\nTrue to trusts and covenants also includes the Reminder that would humble a mountain, the Ark carried as reminder for attentive ears, and the messenger reciting to bring from darkness to light. Trusts are what the pen writes when you are true, and covenants are what you keep when you make room and rise when told. In the garden story, the trust was the poor’s rightful entry today; in Medina, it was the emigrants’ shelter; in family, it is nursing compensation and courteous consultation. Keeping them is the practical form of protecting families from Fire and of walking upright, so the trustworthy are not tossed with roaring nor told away with residents of Blaze, but told to eat and drink joyfully.",
  "33": "\"and who are honest in their testimony;\" adds the next trait, a specific form of trust. The verse highlights testimony as a pillar of the excepted.\n\n“and who are honest in their testimony;” translates “wa alladhina hum bi-shahadatihim qa’imun,” those who stand up with their testimonies, upholding them. Honest, or “qa’imun,” is to stand firm, not lean like worthless planks that impress then collapse. Testimony here is bearing true testimony for Allah as with the two reliable men to witness retaining or separating, and with the pen and what everyone writes that preserves truth.\n\nThis follows true to trusts and covenants to show that testimony is the public act of trust, where the heart’s knowledge that none should feel secure from punishment becomes a stood testimony.\n\nHonesty in testimony counters the despicable oath-taker, slanderer, gossip-monger, withholder, transgressor, brute, whose testimony is false shields that hinder from the Way, whose wealth and children made them say ancient fables and whose snout will be marked.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit testimony to court. Testimony includes daily witness, from divorce documentation to Friday presence, to protecting families.\n\nFor practice, stand with testimony, as the righteous will stand on the Day the earth and mountains are lifted and crushed, the sky torn, Throne borne, and presentation with no secrets hidden occurs. Honest testimony now leads to being among those in Gardens, held in honour, told to eat and drink joyfully, rather than among those whose witnesses are false and whose Flame rips scalps.\n\nHonest testimony also stands on the Day the earth and mountains are lifted and crushed, the sky torn frail, angels on sides, Throne borne by eight, and you are presented with no secret hidden. Standing with testimony then is possible only if you stood with it now when fully capable to prostrate. Honest testimony now is what the pen writes as truth, what the two witnesses bear for Allah in divorce, and what you bear when you are called to remember the Name of your Lord and devote yourself wholeheartedly. Standing then is honour in Gardens, not disgrace with eyes downcast, so the honest are not summoned by Laza that rips scalps but welcomed where fruit hangs within reach.",
  "34": "\"and who are ˹properly˺ observant of their prayers.\" completes the traits of the excepted from impatience, returning to prayer as it began. After listing consistent prayer, rightful share, belief in the Day, fear, guarding chastity, trusts, and testimony, the Quran closes with proper observance.\n\n“and who are properly observant of their prayers” translates “wa alladhina hum ‘ala salatihim yuhafizun,” those who guard, protect, and preserve their prayers. Hafizun is to watch over, maintain conditions, times, and humility, as with protecting families from Fire and guarding chastity. Proper observance includes being mindful to best ability, hearing and obeying, and being held in honour in Gardens, where they are not told away.\n\nThis follows “honest in their testimony” to show that testimony and prayer are twinned; both are standings that preserve truth. Earlier “consistently performing their prayers” began the list; “properly observant” ends it, forming a bracket that encloses wealth, belief, fear, and guarding. The bracket indicates prayer frames the whole character; without it, the other traits lose anchor, and with it, impatience is healed.\n\nProper observance counters the one who crawls facedown, the one who rushes headlong in groups to mock, and the one who expects Garden of Bliss without prayer, as next questions expose.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit observance to outward form. Proper includes time, humility, and effect, as with weighty revelation soon sent and night worship more impactful.\n\nFor practice, be properly observant, not just occasionally performing. Keep prayers as trusts, as you keep covenants, so the punishment that none should feel secure from is feared rightly, and the Gardens where these will be held in honour becomes your honour, where the Day’s molten sky and wool mountains do not terrify the properly observant.\n\nAdditional reflection: Proper observance also means guarding prayer times even when distressed by evil or tempted to withhold good, linking consistent prayer to the rightful share and honest testimony, completing the chain that holds the believer in Gardens held in honour where impatience is healed and disgrace never covers downcast eyes.",
  "35": "\"These will be in Gardens, held in honour.\" states the reward after listing the complete traits of the excepted from impatient creation. The verse is the counterpart to the raging Flame that rips scalps and summons hoarders.\n\n“These will be in Gardens, held in honour” translates “ula’ika fi jannatin mukramun,” those will be in Gardens honoured. Gardens, or “jannat,” are the same Gardens of Bliss with their Lord promised to the righteous, the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach and they are told to eat and drink joyfully, and the splendid homes in Gardens of Eternity. Held in honour, or “mukramun,” indicates they are guests honoured, not merely admitted, as with honour belonging to Allah, His Messenger, and believers, and as with never-ending reward and outstanding character for the Prophet. Honour contrasts with the disgrace that covers downcast eyes when the Shin is bared and with the mark on the snout.\n\nWill be, or future tense, is certain, as with the Inevitable Hour that will have come to pass when Trumpet is blown and earth and mountains are crushed, and as with the Day fifty thousand years that the deniers see as far.\n\nThese are those who were consistently praying, giving rightful share, firmly believing, fearing, guarding, true to trusts, honest in testimony, and properly observant, the opposite of those who withheld when touched with good and gathered and hoarded.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think Gardens held in honour are merely larger orchards. Gardens are honour itself, nearness to the Lord of pathways, the King, Holy, who holds birds.\n\nFor reflection, these will be in Gardens is the answer to “Does every one of them expect to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?” The answer is no, not every rusher, but these, the properly observant. To be among these, move from impatient halu‘ to patient beauty, from distress and withholding to prayer and sharing, so the exception becomes your description and honour becomes your residence.",
  "36": "\"So what is the matter with the disbelievers that they rush ˹head-long˺ towards you ˹O Prophet˺,\" asks after describing those held in honour in Gardens. The question turns to the mockers’ behaviour observed around the Prophet.\n\n“So what is the matter with the disbelievers that they rush headlong towards you O Prophet” translates “fa-ma li-lladhina kafaru qibalaka muhtihin,” what is with those who disbelieve before you, rushing, craning necks. Muhtihin is to rush, to stare with outstretched necks, to crowd in groups to mock, like the eyes that would almost cut you down when they hear the Reminder and say “He is a madman,” like the groups from right and left to mock next, and like the planks that think every cry against them. Rushing headlong indicates not seeking guidance, but surrounding to intimidate, to find fault, to demand punishment bound to come.\n\nThis follows Gardens held in honour to contrast the two movements. The excepted move about the smoothed earth to eat provision and are gathered to Gardens; the disbelievers rush headlong toward the Messenger who recites, not to hear and obey, but to mock, similar to the challenger who demanded punishment and to those who felt secure that One in heaven will not swallow.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read rushing as eager seeking of truth. The context “in groups to mock” clarifies motive. Rushing here is not the beautiful patience commanded nor the beautiful proceeding to Friday remembrance, but curious hostility.\n\nFor believers, the lesson is to not rush headlong toward the Messenger to mock, but to proceed diligently to remembrance and leave business, to be among those who pray consistently and are true to trusts, so rushing is replaced by walking upright on the Straight Path, not crawling facedown, and the Gardens held in honour are approached, not the raging Flame that summons.",
  "37": "\"from the right and the left, in groups ˹to mock you˺?\" details the rushing just questioned. The verse completes the image of disbelievers crowding the Prophet.\n\n“from the right and the left, in groups to mock you” translates “‘an al-yamini wa ‘an al-shimali ‘izin,” from the right and the left in separate groups, bands. ‘Izin are scattered groups, factions, as with “which powerless force will help instead of the Most Compassionate? They are lost in delusion” where delusion splits, and with hypocrites whose malice for each other is intense though you think they are united, yet hearts are divided, and with the despicable’s slanderer, gossip-monger, withholder, transgressor, brute traits that form a band.\n\nFrom the right and left shows encircling, surrounding the Messenger who stands reciting the Reminder to the whole world, the noble Messenger not insane, of outstanding character. Groups to mock indicates the gathering is not for listening and reasoning, as with attentive ears that grasp the Ark reminder, but for mocking, similar to eyes cutting down and saying “He is a madman,” and to calling revelations ancient fables because blessed with wealth and children.\n\nThis follows rushing headlong to specify formation, and precedes “Does every one of them expect to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?” showing that grouping to mock is accompanied by expectation of reward without the traits of the excepted.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think grouping is innocent socializing. Grouping here is organized mockery, like secret talks that grieve, whispering to exclude poor, and promising to leave and help then fleeing.\n\nFor practice, avoid grouping from right and left to mock. Instead, be among those who make room when told, who love emigrants and prefer them, whose private talk is for goodness and righteousness, whose testimony is honest, and whose prayer is properly observed, so grouping becomes the solid ranks loved by Allah like a concrete structure, not the scattered groups that rush to mock and that will be covered with disgrace on the Day they come forth racing to an idol, eyes downcast.",
  "38": "\"Does every one of them expect to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?\" questions the expectation behind rushing to mock. The verse follows the description of groups from right and left.\n\n“Does every one of them expect to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?” translates “a-yatama‘u kullu imri’in minhum an yudkhala jannata na‘im,” does each person among them hope to be entered into the Garden of delight. Garden of Bliss, or “jannat na‘im,” is the same promised to the righteous who will have Gardens of Bliss with their Lord, to the excepted held in honour, and to those who believe and do good for ever and ever. Expect here is “yatama‘,” covet, hope with self-assurance, as with the challenger demanding punishment and the garden owners trusting harvest without “Allah willing,” and as with those who have a scripture reading they will have whatever they choose and oaths binding until the Day.\n\nEvery one of them indicates each mocker individually, not just the group, showing personal accountability, as with every soul detained for what it has done except the people of the right, and as with you will be presented and none of your secrets will stay hidden.\n\nThis follows rushing to mock to expose contradiction. They rush to mock the very Messenger whose Garden they claim to expect, whose outstanding character and never-ending reward they dismiss as madness.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear this as denial that Garden is hoped for. Hope is commanded, as with “We trust our Lord will give us better, for we are turning with hope” and “Our Lord, perfect our light and forgive us.” The denial is of expectation without the traits of the excepted, who pray consistently, give rightful share, believe firmly, fear, guard, keep trusts, and observe prayers.\n\nFor reflection, ask the same of yourself. Do you expect the Garden of Bliss while withholding when touched with good, distressed without patience when touched with evil, and rushing to mock? Replace expectation with preparation, being among those who walk upright, so expectation becomes the modest hope of the garden owners after turning, rewarded with Gardens held in honour.",
  "39": "\"But no! Indeed, they ˹already˺ know what We created them from.\" answers the expectation of Garden without qualification. The response is emphatic denial tied to origin.\n\n“But no! Indeed, they already know what We created them from” translates “kalla inna khalaqnahum mimma ya‘lamun,” No! We created them from what they know. What they know is the despised fluid, the sperm-drop emitted, the clot, the creation in stages, the “We created you yet some are disbelievers while some are believers” and “He shaped you perfecting form,” and “Were they not once a sperm-drop emitted? Then a clinging clot, then He developed and perfected form.” They already know they were created from this low, dependent beginning, not from independent honour that expels inferior.\n\nBut no, or “kalla,” is the same emphatic no as for ransom with everyone on earth, “But no! There will certainly be a raging Flame,” and for treating submitted like wicked, “Should We treat submitted like wicked? What is the matter with you?” No here denies that mere expectation grants Gardens.\n\n“Indeed, they already know what We created them from” reminds them that Gardens are not for those who forget origin and think honour belongs to themselves. The Garden of Bliss is for those who remember they were created from what they know and therefore are humble, in awe without seeing, give thanks hardly given, and are mindful to best ability.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read “what We created them from” as insult to creation, as if the drop is shameful. The drop is honoured as the beginning of the soul that will be presented with no secret hidden, but it humbles those who boast like planks that impress and like brutes with wealth and children who say ancient fables, reminding them they were not created from different substance than the submitted.\n\nFor practice, remember your origin when you rush to mock or expect Bliss without effort. The smoothed earth you move about and the hearing, sight, intellect you hardly thank all came from that drop shaped perfectly. That remembrance should make you walk humbly upright, not rush headlong to mock, and should make you among those whose Gardens are held in honour because they remembered creation and were grateful.",
  "40": "\"So, I do swear by the Lord of ˹all˺ the points of sunrise and sunset that We are truly capable\" is a solemn oath after denying that every mocker will enter the Garden of Bliss. The verse uses the Lord of sun’s movements to assert capability.\n\n“So, I do swear by the Lord of all the points of sunrise and sunset that We are truly capable” translates “fa-la uqsimu bi-rabbi al-mashariqi wa al-magharibi inna la-qadirun,” I swear by the Lord of the easts and wests. Points of sunrise and sunset, or “mashariq wa magharib,” are the multiple rising and setting places of the sun across seasons, witnessed by all who move about the earth eating provision and who look again at flawless heavens without finding flaw. Lord of them is the King, Holy, who smoothed earth and adorned lowest heaven with lamps, who merges night into day, and who holds all authority, Most Capable of everything.\n\nWe are truly capable, or “la-qadirun,” is the same capability that holds birds, makes water sink then brings flowing water, creates seven heavens in layers, revives dead earth, makes gardens ashes, and can replace them with others better than them, as next verse states and none can be prevented. Capability here specifically covers replacing mockers with better peoples and resurrecting as with reassembling fingertips, as with “Is such a Creator unable to bring the dead back to life?” and “Yes, by my Lord, you will surely be resurrected and that is easy.”\n\nThis oath follows origin from what they know, showing that the One who created you from a drop and set your appointed time that never delays can also replace you.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat points of sunrise as mere geography. They are signs that the Inevitable’s Day, when sky is molten brass and mountains wool, is within the same Lord’s capability.\n\nFor reflection, seeing daily sunrise and sunset from different points should recall that the Lord of those points can replace a mocking, rushing, withholding generation with one that prays consistently, gives rightful share, and walks upright, so capability becomes invitation to be among those held in honour, not among those replaced.",
  "41": "\"of replacing them with ˹others˺ better than them, and We cannot be prevented ˹from doing so˺.\" completes the oath by the Lord of easts and wests. The verse states what capability can do.\n\n“of replacing them with others better than them, and We cannot be prevented from doing so” translates “‘ala an nubaddila khayran minhum wa ma nahnu bi-masbuqin,” to replace with better than them, and We are not to be outrun. Better here means in obedience, generosity, and mindfulness, as with the better wives submissive, faithful, devout, repentant if divorce occurred, and the better garden hoped for after turning, and the better people after expulsion without horse or camel. Better than mockers are those who make room, love emigrants, prefer them despite need, and are saved from selfishness and truly successful.\n\n“We cannot be prevented” translates “ma nahnu bi-masbuqin,” We are not to be preceded or escaped, similar to “We have ordained death and We cannot be prevented from transforming you” and “We are truly capable, and none can be prevented.” It echoes that punishment for disbelievers is averted by none from the Lord of pathways and that no close friend will ask on the Day. Prevention is impossible because the Most Capable holds all authority and descends command between seven heavens, and Treasuries belong to Him yet hypocrites do not comprehend.\n\nThis follows capability oath to show that Garden expectation without traits is futile; the Lord can and will replace the unqualified with qualified.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear replacement as ethnic displacement. Better is by best deeds, not by tribe, as He created you and some are disbelievers while some are believers and He is All-Seeing of what you do, and as the submitted are not treated like the wicked.\n\nFor practice, be among the better, so you are not replaced. Be among those who pray, give, believe, fear, guard, keep trusts, testify honestly, and observe prayers, whose Gardens are held in honour, so the oath by easts and wests finds you among those truly capable’s favoured, not among those indulging until they face their Day which they have been threatened with.",
  "42": "\"So let them indulge ˹in falsehood˺ and amuse ˹themselves˺ until they face their Day, which they have been threatened with—\" instructs the Prophet after the oath that the Lord can replace mockers with better. The verse gives temporary rein, not approval.\n\n“So let them indulge in falsehood and amuse themselves until they face their Day, which they have been threatened with—” translates “fa-dharhum yakhudu wa yal‘abu hatta yulaqu yawmahum alladhi yu‘adun,” leave them to indulge in vain talk and play until they meet their Day promised. Indulge, or “yakhudu,” is to wade into falsehood, as with those who indulged in falsehood along with others, who denied the Day of Judgment until the inevitable came, and who turned away from the reminder as if spooked zebras fleeing from a lion. Amuse, or “yal‘abu,” is to play, as with worldly life’s play, amusement, luxury, boasting, competition that becomes chaff, and as with fanfare and caravan that drew crowds from the pulpit.\n\nUntil they face their Day, which they have been threatened with, is the appointment that never delays, the Day the disbelievers rush toward mocking, the Day the sky is molten brass, mountains wool, no hamim asks, and the wicked wish to ransom with everyone on earth. It is the Day fifty thousand years they see as far but We see as near, the Day earth and mountains are lifted and crushed, sky torn, Throne borne, and presentation with no secrets hidden.\n\nThis follows capability to replace, showing that replacement is not immediate, but after respite, as with gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend and delay for a while but planning is flawless, and as treasures belong to Allah yet hypocrites do not comprehend, so indulgence continues until appointment.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to take “let them indulge” as permission to ignore. It is leaving them to the consequences while the Prophet continues with beautiful patience, not like Jonah, and continues reciting the Reminder to the whole world.\n\nFor believers, let them indulge is not your indulgence. Disperse to seek bounty and remember often after Friday, be among those in awe without seeing, so your Day is the elevated Garden where fruit hangs within reach and you are told to eat joyfully, not the Day you indulged until you faced the threatened Day with eyes downcast, utterly covered with disgrace.",
  "43": "\"the Day they will come forth from the graves swiftly, as if racing to an idol ˹for a blessing˺,\" describes the threatened Day they indulge until. The verse gives the physical emergence.\n\n“the Day they will come forth from the graves swiftly, as if racing to an idol for a blessing” translates “yawma yakhrujuna min al-ajdathi sira‘an ka-annahum ila nusubin yufidun,” they will exit the graves quickly, hurrying as if to a goal, a stone set up. Sira‘an is racing, as with the Inevitable’s swift crushing and the sky torn frail, and as with garden owners going early fixated. Idol, or “nusub,” is a stone, an object of worship, a goal they once raced to in the world for honour, merchandise, or fanfare. Now they race from graves, not by choice, as they were made to see each other, eyes downcast.\n\nSwiftly contrasts with beautiful patience commanded and with the measured recitation at night. In the world they indulged and amused; now they race without indulgence.\n\nThis follows “until they face their Day” to show facing, and precedes eyes downcast covered with disgrace, showing that racing ends in humiliation.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think racing to an idol is praise. It is simile for speed and direction, showing that the haste they once used for false goals now carries them to judgment.\n\nFor reflection, choose what you race to now. Race to forgiveness and a Paradise as vast as heavens and earth, to the call on Friday where you proceed diligently, to the good loan multiplied, to protecting families. That racing makes the race from graves not toward disgrace but toward Gardens held in honour, where you are not racing to an idol but are told to eat and drink joyfully for days gone by.\n\nAdditional thought: The idol for a blessing they race toward in the simile represents any false object of hope that once drew them—wealth, clan, or merchandise fanfare that made crowds leave the pulpit. Racing now toward graves shows that the object does not save. To ensure your race ends in honour, race now toward the call on Friday where you proceed diligently, toward the good loan multiplied, and toward protecting families, so the only racing from graves you know is toward Gardens held in honour where you are welcomed, not toward an idol that offers no blessing.",
  "44": "\"with eyes downcast, utterly covered with disgrace. That is the Day they have ˹always˺ been warned of.\" closes the Day description after the graves’ swift racing. The verse gives the final look of those who indulged.\n\n“with eyes downcast, utterly covered with disgrace” translates “khashi‘atan absaruhum tarhaquhum dhillah,” eyes humbled, disgrace overwhelming them, as with the Day the Shin is bared and they are asked to prostrate but cannot, eyes downcast covered with disgrace because they were called to prostrate when fully capable but chose not to. Disgrace here is the same that covers when torment draws near and faces become gloomy, when they are told “This is what you claimed would never come,” and when wealth and authority are stripped. Downcast eyes contrast with bright faces looking at their Lord and with the upright walk on the Straight Path.\n\n“That is the Day they have always been warned of” translates “dhalika al-yawm alladhi kanu yu‘adun,” that is the Day they were promised, warned by every warner who came with clear proofs, by Noah, by the Reminder that would humble a mountain, by the Pen and what everyone writes, by the Ark carried as reminder for attentive ears, and by the messenger reciting to bring from darkness. Always warned indicates repeated warning, not surprise, similar to “Have the stories of those before not reached you?” and “So be warned.”\n\nThis follows racing to an idol to show that indulgence and amusement end in disgrace, and that the Day the disbelievers see as far is the Day they have always been warned of.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear disgrace as mere embarrassment. It is the recompense for seeking honour by expelling the inferior and for saying ancient fables, now reversed.\n\nFor the living, avoid downcast disgrace by lifting eyes now to flawless heavens without finding flaw, to birds held, to the smoothed earth, and to the Messenger’s recitation, so the Day you are warned of finds you among those with eyes bright, not downcast, whose Gardens are held in honour and whose light shines ahead, and who are not covered with disgrace but with honour from the Lord of easts and wests who is truly capable."
};